a national monument and symbol of south  african democracy up in flames  a fire tore through cape town's  buildings of parliament sunday severely  damaging the offices of south africa's  ruling party  and later spreading to the national  assembly  south african president cyril ramaphosa  calling it a devastating event  but insisting that the work of  parliament must go on  much as it is disappointing devastating  there is something  that we can be grateful for for these  men and women  in  uniform who  go into the most dangerous places that  you can ever think of risking their limb  and their lives  the devastation occurred on the heels of  the state funeral of archbishop desmond  tutu held a day earlier just streets  away the president and many of south  africa's high-ranking politicians  already in the city to honor the  anti-apartheid hero arch  would have been devastated as well  because this is a place that  he not only supported prayed for  and wanted to see us  the repository  of the democracy that he worked so hard  for  dozens of firefighters spent all sunday  tackling the blaze police and medical  units were also on standby though no  casualties were reported  this is a very sad day for our democracy  because parliament is the home of our  democracy  and parliament is also a strategic key  point the national assembly is a symbol  of south africa's democracy here is  where the president delivers his state  of the nation address and it's also  where nelson mandela was elected as the  first black president of south africa of  course it's a place that attracts  tourists from all around the world  next month president cyril ramaphosa is  expected to deliver that state of the  nation address  many might expect that this will no  longer be the scene  the incident igniting a long-time debate  to move parliament to the country's  capital pretoria  but for a place that serves such a  pivotal role during south africa's  apartheid era that is a move many south  africans feel would strike a painful  chord  jillian wolf al jazeera cape town
 

